body:
Most production bugs I've chased weren't crashes. A crash is loud: someone reports it and it gets fixed. The bugs that worry me are the quiet ones, where a request fails, nothing tells the user, and the page just looks a little wrong.

This year I audited the error handling in the frontend of a multi-customer SaaS product I work on: a Next.js app with a backend-for-frontend (BFF) layer and single sign-on. I ended up with 15 findings: 3 critical, 4 high, 5 medium and 3 low. These are the patterns that mattered most, and the fixes I proposed.

## How I ran the audit

I used an AI agent to sweep the codebase: every `fetch` call, every API route, every error handler and the whole token-refresh flow. Then I went through what it found, checked each finding against the code, dropped the false alarms, and sorted the rest by real user impact.

For each finding I wrote down four things: where it is, what goes wrong, a concrete scenario a user could hit, and the fix with an effort estimate. The scenario is the part that turns a code smell into something a team will actually prioritise.

## 1. Requests with no timeout

Most server routes called `fetch` with no timeout. If the CMS or the identity provider got slow, the request just waited, and so did the user. Under load, hung requests pile up on the server until everything slows down.

One route already did this right, so the fix was to pull that pattern into a shared helper and use it everywhere:

```ts
export async function fetchWithTimeout(
  url: string,
  options: RequestInit,
  timeoutMs = 15_000
): Promise<Response> {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const id = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeoutMs);
  try {
    return await fetch(url, { ...options, signal: controller.signal });
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(id);
  }
}
```

> If one place in your codebase already handles a problem well, the fix is usually to make that the only way to do it.

## 2. Errors that lose their meaning

A common pattern threw the HTTP status as a string and converted it back to a number in the `catch` block. That works until something else lands in the `catch`, like a `TypeError` from a null value. Then the conversion returns `NaN`, the user gets a generic 500, and the logs say nothing useful.

The fix is small: throw the status as a number, and treat anything else as an unexpected error.

```ts
try {
  const res = await fetchWithTimeout(url, options);
  if (!res.ok) throw res.status;
  return NextResponse.json(await res.json());
} catch (error) {
  const status = typeof error === "number" ? error : 500;
  return handleError(status);
}
```

## 3. A session that expires without telling you

When the token refresh failed, the app recorded it but showed nothing. The user kept browsing a page that looked normal, then hit an "Unauthorized" message on their next click, sometimes half an hour later.

The proposed fix: as soon as refresh fails, show a clear banner ("Your session has ended. Sign in again.") with the action right there. Sessions expiring is normal. Surprising people with it isn't.

## 4. Forms and search that fail quietly

Three user-facing gaps carried the highest UX risk:

- **Forms:** a failed submit showed no message, so people clicked again and risked sending duplicates. Fix: show the error next to the submit button and disable the button while the request is in flight.
- **Search:** a failed search looked exactly like "no results". Fix: show "Search is temporarily unavailable" so users don't conclude the content doesn't exist.
- **Toasts:** only one toast could show at a time, so when two requests failed together, the first error disappeared. Fix: allow a short queue.

## 5. One broken widget takes down the page

The app had a single global error boundary. A runtime error in one sidebar widget replaced the whole page with an error screen, including content that was working fine.

Wrapping each widget region in its own error boundary keeps failures local. The broken part shows a small "couldn't load" note, and the rest of the page keeps working.

## 6. Requests that outlive their component

Several components started a `fetch` inside `useEffect` without cleaning up. If the user navigated away mid-request, the response still arrived and updated state, which could show stale data when they came back. An `AbortController` in the effect cleanup fixes it:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal })
    .then((r) => r.json())
    .then(setData)
    .catch((e) => {
      if (e.name !== "AbortError") handleError(e);
    });
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [url]);
```

## What I took away

- **Design the failure states with the feature.** Loading, empty, error and expired-session states belong in the spec, not in a polish task for later.
- **Write the user scenario, not just the code smell.** "Users can submit a form twice" gets prioritised. "Missing error state" doesn't.
- **Use AI for coverage, keep the judgment.** The agent read every file faster than I could. Deciding what was real, what mattered and what to fix first was still my job.
- **Make the right pattern the easy one.** Shared helpers for timeouts, errors and boundaries work better than a checklist everyone has to remember.

If you work on a frontend that talks to several backends, try this: pick one user flow, make each dependency slow or fail in turn, and watch what the user sees. That's usually where the quiet bugs are.
